# Gate step 1273-4mak — data, not code (order 1072-b7eq).
#
# WHAT IT GUARDS. The curl-install smoke — the acceptance gate a release is
# promoted to stable on — verified INTEGRITY and never AUTHENTICITY. Measured by
# the row's author 2026-09-19: `grep -ci 'cosign|verify.sh' SKILL.md` returned
# ZERO on every lane. Each release publishes a .cosign.bundle beside every asset
# (v56.9.19.2: 32 assets, each bundled, plus verify.sh and its own bundle) and
# nothing read one, ever.
#
# WHY A HASH IS NOT A SIGNATURE, which ARM 3 exists to make concrete rather than
# argue: install.sh checks the asset's SHA256 against a SHA256SUMS fetched from
# the same place as the asset. Substitute the asset, regenerate the manifest
# entry, and the pair is self-consistent — `sha256sum -c` PASSES. ARM 3 builds
# exactly that pair, proves the hash check passes on it, and requires the lane to
# refuse on the signature.
#
# MEASURED WHILE WRITING THIS, and it sharpened the fix: SHA256SUMS and
# SHA256SUMS-macos have NO cosign bundle; only SHA256SUMS-windows does. So
# "verify the manifest and trust every asset it lists" is unavailable on two of
# three lanes, and the blocks verify THE ARTIFACT THE LANE INSTALLED against its
# own bundle instead.
#
# THE FIXTURE RUNS THE RUNBOOK'S OWN TEXT. It extracts the Linux block from §1s
# of SKILL.md and executes it against a fake release over file:// with a stub
# cosign. A fixture that re-implemented the block would verify its own paraphrase
# and let the runbook rot underneath it; ARM 0 fails loudly if the block cannot
# be extracted or stops probing for cosign.
#
# THE STUB MODELS A SIGNATURE, NOT A VERDICT: it records the signed bytes at
# setup and exits 0 only while the artifact still matches them. ARM 3 therefore
# fails for the reason a real signature fails, not because a stub was told to.
# ARM 2 is its counterweight — a genuine artifact must still verify, or a block
# that refused everything would pass ARMs 1 and 3 and be useless.
#
# NO REAL-COSIGN ARM, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. cosign is absent on this
# host, in the toolbox, and from dnf (yoga, 2026-09-20); macneo measured the same
# absence on macOS. The row's own criterion 2 is that could-not-run is never a
# pass, and that applies to its test: ARM 1 pins the could-not-run path, which is
# the path EVERY host in this fleet takes today. When a host gains cosign, a real
# arm belongs here and ARM 1 stays.
#
# COST, MEASURED HERE 2026-09-20: 0.05 / 0.05 s. No network — the fake release is
# served over file:// — no container, no compile.
#
# STEP_SCRIPT is a LITERAL path on purpose: a binding assembled from a variable
# is invisible to every name-based scan, including the bound-or-retired guard
# (1063-nraf). Keep it a plain string.
STEP_DESC="Checking the smoke verifies a signature and never reads could-not-run as a pass (1273-4mak)"
STEP_SCRIPT="scripts/test-smoke-verifies-a-signature.sh"
STEP_ERROR="the release acceptance smoke can again promote a release without verifying any signature, or a host without cosign can report a verified one (1273-4mak) — read which arm failed above; ARM 0 failing means §1s was removed from the runbook and no lane verifies anything"
STEP_OK="The smoke verifies a signature; could-not-run is not a pass"
